        "date": "2026-11-14T00:53:22Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(import): judge a closed shell's seams face by face; refuse only a real gap\n\nThe first closed-shell law counted every edge not used exactly twice in\nopposite directions as open, and refused two healthy CAD exports. Each\npass now keeps the facet range of every face, and the seams between faces\nare told apart:\n\n- a face wound against its neighbours (a shared edge running the same\n  way twice) is flipped to agree with them, and each connected group is\n  turned to enclose a positive volume; logged, not refused;\n- a boundary split differently on each side (T-junctions) is closed when\n  every boundary edge's ends and midpoint lie within two chord tolerances\n  of another face's boundary;\n- only a boundary edge farther than that from every other face is a gap,\n  refused with the face that owns most of them and the widest distance.\n\nOpen shells keep the kernel's winding and only refuse a dropped face.\n\nRefs #1573\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX",
